WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.850
Asha's checkout times out.

00:00:02.738 --> 00:00:06.388
Platform threads stay occupied waiting for responses.

00:00:07.275 --> 00:00:10.212
When the request thread pool fills, new shoppers

00:00:10.238 --> 00:00:10.512
queue.

00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:14.096
Virtual threads are lightweight.

00:00:14.996 --> 00:00:19.046
During supported network waits, Java frees the underlying

00:00:19.096 --> 00:00:21.821
operating system thread for other requests.

00:00:22.671 --> 00:00:24.796
More checkouts can progress together.

00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:29.071
This helps when threads were the bottleneck.

00:00:29.958 --> 00:00:31.446
Payment is not faster.

00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:35.771
Twenty database connections still allow only twenty

00:00:35.771 --> 00:00:37.258
connections at once.

00:00:38.867 --> 00:00:40.617
Save one payment attempt.

00:00:41.517 --> 00:00:43.879
Repeated clicks reuse its reference.

00:00:44.754 --> 00:00:47.217
A timeout does not prove payment failed.

00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:52.083
A bulkhead bounds active calls and waiting work.

00:00:53.008 --> 00:00:56.071
Give busy guidance only before payment starts.

00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:59.196
Keep database transactions short.

00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:02.646
Set network deadlines.

00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:07.083
For uncertain payment, show checking and query the

00:01:07.183 --> 00:01:10.158
original provider reference before confirming.

00:01:11.046 --> 00:01:12.796
Never blindly pay again.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.375
Virtual threads improve waiting efficiency.

00:01:18.238 --> 00:01:20.700
Capacity limits protect dependencies.

00:01:21.562 --> 00:01:24.062
Safe recovery protects Asha's payment.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:26.550
Checkout needs all three.
